# --- Web (dev) ---
# Vite dev server port; the dev API attaches at API_PORT and Vite proxies /api.
PORT=3000
API_PORT=3001
# Public origin the app is served from. Locally: http://localhost:3000.
# Production: your public origin, e.g. https://bins.example.org. QR codes
# carry this origin (they're generated from the page's own location).
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Server ---
# Production only: unix socket the Bun server binds (Caddy proxies to it).
#SOCKET_PATH=/run/bins/bins.sock
# SQLite database file. Created + migrated automatically on boot.
DATABASE_PATH=./data/bins.db
# Directory for content-addressed photo blobs.
PHOTOS_PATH=./data/photos

# First-boot: no env needed — visiting the app on an empty database opens the
# /setup page (group name, landing branding, access code, admin password).
# Additional groups: bun scripts/create-group.ts

# --- Credential recovery ---
# The group access code is stored readable and printed at startup, so a
# forgotten one can always be recovered from the server log. It is also shown
# in /admin, and `bun scripts/reset-credentials.ts` can set a new access code
# or admin password WITHOUT knowing the old one (no data is touched).
# The trade: anyone who can read the log or the database can join the group.
# That is close to what the code already implies — every joined device caches
# it locally in plaintext to build invite links — but set this to 0 if your
# logs go somewhere you don't control. The admin password is NEVER logged and
# is only ever stored hashed.
#LOG_ACCESS_CODE=0

# --- Trust model ---
# Set this ONLY when the deployment sits behind a network perimeter (a LAN, a
# VPN) enforced by the reverse proxy. It removes the need for sticker secrets:
# joining takes just a display name, and allocated stickers get a bare
# `/{id}` QR instead of `/{id}#CODE`. Single-group deployments only.
#OPEN_ACCESS=1
# Backstop for a proxy misconfiguration: open joins are refused unless the
# forwarded client address is private. On by default with OPEN_ACCESS; the
# perimeter is still the proxy's job, this only stops a bad rule from being an
# instantly open door. Set to 0 for dev (no proxy sets the header) or for a
# perimeter that hands out public addresses.
#OPEN_ACCESS_REQUIRE_PRIVATE_CLIENT=0
# The backstop only recognises private IPv4, unique-local and link-local
# IPv6 — a phone on a dual-stack LAN arrives from a GLOBAL IPv6 address and
# would be refused. The reverse proxy knows better (it can test on-link), so
# it may vouch for a request with `header_up X-Bins-Perimeter lan` inside
# its LAN-only block; bins honours that header and nothing else can set it.
# Let admins in from OUTSIDE the perimeter, with a passkey and nothing else.
# For a perimeter-protected deployment whose proxy is widened to forward
# outsiders too — WITHOUT the perimeter header (`header_up -X-Bins-Perimeter`
# on the external path; the header is how bins tells the two apart). Off the
# perimeter, every API call then needs a device with a live passkey session:
# no join of any kind, no setup, no integration token. A fresh browser signs
# in with a passkey and gets a device minted for it. Unset = no distinction.
#REMOTE_ACCESS=passkey

# --- Home surface ---
# Which screen "/" opens on. Independent of the trust model above.
#   scanner (default) — the camera IS the home screen. Right when the job is
#                       "work through this pile of boxes": scan, snap, next.
#   browse            — open on the box list + search, with Scan one prominent
#                       tap away (/scan). Right for a standing warehouse, where
#                       the question is "which box is it in".
#   shelves           — open on the wall: every bay, shelf and slot with the
#                       box in it. Right where the shelving IS the index and
#                       people navigate by where things are.
#HOME_VIEW=browse

# --- Box numbers ---
# Is the box number meaningful to the people using this deployment?
#   public (default) — printed on the container, said out loud ("grab bin 47").
#                      It leads the UI.
#   internal         — containers get relabeled and reused, so the id is just a
#                      URL handle; the box's NAME leads instead and the number
#                      is a quiet fallback for boxes not yet named.
# Presentation only — ids stay integers from one monotonic sequence either way,
# which is what guarantees they're never reused (so a leftover sticker always
# resolves to its own dead record, never to someone else's inventory).
#BOX_NUMBERS=internal

# --- Notifications (optional) ---
# Web push, so an admin hears about a suggested edit without opening the app.
# Suggestions are the only thing that notifies: everything else a member can
# change applies itself, so there's nothing waiting on a human.
# Generate ONCE per deployment and keep them:  bun scripts/generate-vapid.ts
# All three must be set or push is simply off (no button, no subscriptions).
# The private key is a credential — treat it like the admin password.
# Rotating the pair invalidates every existing subscription.
#VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
#VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# A contact the push service can reach about this deployment (RFC 8292):
# mailto: or an https URL.
#VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.org

# --- Label printing ---
# Where the finished label PNG goes. bins renders the label (title, subtext,
# line art, QR) itself, so the printer only has to print an image:
#   ipp://host[:631]/path   an IPP printer (anything that does AirPrint, most
#                           network label printers): one Print-Job per copy
#                           with document-format image/png. No shim needed.
#   http(s)://...           anything that accepts `POST image/png`.
# Unset = no "Print" button; previews and artwork still work, and the
# sticker-code export stays the path pre-printed-sticker operators use.
#LABEL_PRINT_URL=ipp://labelprinter:631/ipp/print
# Optional bearer. Server-side only — never sent to a browser.
#LABEL_PRINT_TOKEN=

# --- Label art (optional) ---
# Generated black-and-white line art for labels, from Google's Gemini image
# models. Entirely optional: with no key set, labels render as text + QR and
# nothing calls out to anyone. This is the only part of the system that
# spends real money per action. Make a key for this app in Google AI Studio.
#GEMINI_API_KEY=
# The default model. The new-box screen offers Lite / Flash / Pro
# (gemini-3.1-flash-lite-image $0.0336, gemini-3.1-flash-image $0.067,
# gemini-3-pro-image $0.134) with the cost shown; this is the one preselected.
# Also priced: gemini-2.5-flash-image ($0.039), gemini-3-pro-image-preview
# ($0.134), imagen-4.0-generate-001 ($0.04), imagen-4.0-ultra-generate-001
# ($0.06) — Imagen models take no reference pictures.
#LABEL_ART_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-lite-image
# Monthly ceiling in USD. Unset means NO ceiling — set one deliberately.
# Checked before each call; images already generated are always reusable.
#LABEL_ART_BUDGET_USD=5.00
# Where generated art is cached (keyed by model+prompt, so regenerating the
# same box is free). Defaults to a sibling of PHOTOS_PATH.
#LABEL_ART_PATH=./data/art
